WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.428 --> 00:00:01.970
STEPHANIE WONG: Now,
it's quite clear

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.400
that you won't migrate
your entire on-prem

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.200
to the cloud in the first go.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.210
It makes a lot more
sense to migrate portions

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.160
at a time where appropriate.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.710
As such, you'll still need a
way for your on-prem systems

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.620
to communicate with your
newly minted cloud resource.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.090
In this episode,
I'm going to dive

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.190
into Google Cloud
Interconnect so you can set up

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:22.340
high-speed direct connection
to Google's network for faster

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.130
data migration and an
optimal hybrid environment.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.060
Stay tuned.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:33.930
Sure, you can reach
your cloud resources

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.180
with the appropriate VPC
firewalls and IPs in place.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.600
But if your hybrid architecture
requires connections

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.330
with higher availability
and lower latency,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.190
then a direct connection
to Google's network

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.290
can offer more reliability
and performance.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.680
So let's look at how this
plays out in reality.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.480
Black Friday is
coming up and you're

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.090
expecting a significant
spike in compute resources

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.820
to support the inbound
traffic to your site.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.940
Now, if you have your
compute in a GCP-VPC

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.180
then you could directly ping
them from your on-prem system.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.940
This would work, technically.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.840
You'd get scalable
systems in the cloud

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.910
and secure processing on prem.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.250
On the backside, however,
there are a load of issues.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.030
Firstly, this forces the
communication between your VPC

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.790
and on-prem to go through
the public internet,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.030
communicating through publicly
visible IPs, most likely

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.460
requiring you to secure
your traffic via a VPN

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.420
in the middle.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.180
Secondly, this produces
a significant performance

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.110
challenge.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.810
The public internet is
not the most performant,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.970
and the extra overhead
of VPN on the packets

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.220
could lead to less than
desired performance

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.980
on a significant
shopping holiday.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.080
What you want instead
is a reliable way

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.790
to connect your
on-premise workloads

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.940
to the public cloud in a secure,
fast, and reliable manner.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.730
In other words,
you want to extend

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.860
your on-premise private
network into Google

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.020
Cloud over a dedicated link.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.790
The purpose of Google
Cloud Interconnect

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.920
is to provide direct
fast connection

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.530
between your on-premise
and Google Cloud networks

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.120
in a secure fashion.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.180
This security and performance
mixture is hyper-important

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.060
for a large number of industries
who work between on-prem

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.170
and in cloud--

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.670
for example, data migration,
replication, disaster recovery,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.550
or other high-performance
computing situations.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.550
In these scenarios, you want
to directly connect your cloud

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.828
systems while extending
your private on-prem network

00:02:19.828 --> 00:02:20.370
to the cloud.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.060
Google Cloud Interconnect
provides a few options that

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.840
can suit your specific needs.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.220
But the one I'll focus on
is Dedicated Interconnect,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.290
which provides direct
physical connections

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.590
between your on-premise
network and Google's network.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.160
It works like this.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.580
You set up a cross connect
between your own router

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.330
and the Google network at a
common co-location facility.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.640
If you can't perform this,
then the official docs

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.170
have some options for you.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.630
A BGP session is configured
over the interconnect

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.330
between the Cloud router
and your on-premise router.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.940
BGP is a protocol that
helps route traffic

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.000
to subnets using the shortest
paths between edge routers.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.770
Traffic from your
on-premise network

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.040
can reach your Google
Cloud VPC and vice versa.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.620
With Dedicated Interconnect,
the immediate benefit

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.420
is an enterprise
grade connection

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.200
to your Google VPC with a
dedicated 10-gigabit per second

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.900
circuit directly to
a Google location.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.150
Moreover, you now
have connectivity

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.240
reaching beyond Google's
existing network locations,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.790
allowing you to scale
connectivity and save

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.490
on your egress traffic
costs from your VPC network

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.913
to your on-premise network.

00:03:25.913 --> 00:03:28.080
Now you have a way to
transfer large amounts of data

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.970
between networks,
which can be more

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.430
cost effective than purchasing
additional bandwidth

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.500
over the public internet.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.420
On top of that,
you'll experience

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.260
fewer disruptions and drops with
a predictable user experience.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.780
Traffic between your
on-premise and VPC networks

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.580
doesn't touch the
public internet,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.580
meaning fewer hops and potential
points of failure, where

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.260
it can be disrupted or dropped.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.230
A key benefit is that your
VPC's internal IP addresses

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.720
can be directly accessed
from your on-premise network

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.730
with peering.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.510
No net device or
VPN tunnel required.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.350
Now let's dive into how to
actually get that set up.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.750
First, let's make sure
you have a VPC set up

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.350
for your cloud environment.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.250
If you haven't set that up,
see the previous episode.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.710
Now, the first steps in
using Cloud Interconnect

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.860
involve deciding if you want
to use a dedicated connection

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.399
or a partner connection.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.860
While there are few
nuances between the two,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.050
depending on your
needs, the primary ones

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.960
that I'm concerned
with are speed.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.660
Dedicated Interconnect
is ideal for situations

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.510
where you need more than
a 10-gigabit connection.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.330
A Partner can be used
for lower speed needs.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.330
Access-- if your
on-prem can't physically

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.000
meet Google's network in
a co-location facility,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.040
then you can use
Partner Interconnect.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:43.590
Now, for the sake
of our demo, we're

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.270
going to use
Dedicated Interconnect

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.340
because it's a little
simpler and can be cheaper

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.300
in some situations.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.350
Let's see what this looks like.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.320
Go to the Cloud Interconnect
Physical Connections tab

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.150
in the Google Cloud
Platform console.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.660
Select Setup Connection.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.420
Select Dedicated Interconnect,
and then select Continue.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.300
Now select Order new
Dedicated Interconnect,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.230
and then select Continue.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.010
Specify the details for your
name, location, and capacity

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.910
of the interconnect.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.180
Now, capacity is an
interesting metric.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.950
The capacity you
need is determined

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.860
by the number of 10-gigabit
per second connections you can

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.830
order, also called a circuit.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.420
Now select Next.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.942
We'll skip over the redundancy
information for now.

00:05:31.942 --> 00:05:33.650
But if you need that,
please see the docs

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:36.270
linked in the description below.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:41.320
Specify your contact information
and review your order.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.460
Now select Place Order.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.830
On the Order Confirmation
page, review the next steps,

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.140
and then select Done.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.960
Now, it's important to note
that you are in fact ordering

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.060
a dedicated interconnect
connection from your

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.250
on prem to Google itself.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.260
Once Google's done
allocating things,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.090
it will send you a
confirmation email,

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.730
and send you some
LOA-CFAs, which

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.465
you'll send to your vendor.

00:06:04.465 --> 00:06:06.840
They will provision the cross
connects between the Google

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.370
Peering Edge and you're
on-premises network.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.430
Now, once this is
all set up, Google

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.530
has to test your
access extensively

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:17.470
before you'll be able to use
the Interconnect directly.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.900
There are some complicated
steps involved with this that

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.430
are specific to your setup.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.500
So we'll point you to
the resource linked

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.500
in the description below
to do that, and skip it

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.600
for this video.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.880
Once all that is
done, your final step

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:33.220
is to configure your
VLAN attachments.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.550
Once Google verifies you
have a cross connect,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.250
you see that your interconnect
is ready to configure.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:43.660
And you can click Finish Setup,
and select Add VLAN Attachment.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:44.560
Give it a name--

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.120
My Attachment-- and
select or create

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:51.360
a cloud router to associate
with this attachment.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.640
It has to be in the VPC network
that you want to connect to.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.830
After you're done adding
the VLAN attachments,

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.360
select Create.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.470
The attachment takes a
few moments to create.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:06.590
Now you can click Configure to
add a BGP session to your cloud

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.390
router's interface.

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:11.800
You give it a name
for the BGP session,

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.830
the public or private ASN of
your on-premises router, which

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:18.160
uniquely identifies each
network on the internet for BGP

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.400
sessions, and an optional
advertised route priority.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.190
The Cloud router and
on-premises BGP-IP addresses

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:26.530
are already allocated
by the VLAN attachment.

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:30.070
The BGP sessions are inactive
until you configure a BGP

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:32.560
on your on-premises router.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.530
If you're building redundancy
with a duplicate interconnect,

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:37.780
repeat these steps for
the second interconnect.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.940
And specify a
different Cloud router.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.440
For more info, check
out the documentation.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.130
Now, I want to note there
are few nuances between how

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:48.490
you use Cloud VPN,
VPC, and Dedicated

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.050
Interconnect with your own VPN.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.300
But that's all covered quite
well in the official doc,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.665
so again, I'll direct you
there in the description.

00:07:55.665 --> 00:07:57.790
Now that you have configured
a dedicated connection

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.650
to Google's network,
the next step

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.270
is to protect your
cloud instances

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:02.980
by configuring firewall rules.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:04.480
If you want to learn
how to do that,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.120
then stay tuned for the next
episode of "Google Cloud

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.870
Networking End-to-End."

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:10.690
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.760
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:15.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

